  "rule": {
    "title": "be able to · -ing as a subject",
    "explanation": "«Can» solo tiene presente («can») y pasado («could»). Para los demás tiempos se usa «be able to»:\n- «I'll be able to buy a car.» (podré)\n- «I haven't been able to sleep.» (no he podido dormir)\n- «I'd like to be able to speak Irish.»\n- «I might be able to help you.»\n\nPara un logro concreto en el pasado, con esfuerzo, se usa «was» / «were able to» (no «could»). En negativa valen los dos:\n- «After three hours, I was able to finish the exam.»\n- «I couldn't find it.» / «I wasn't able to find it.»\n\nEl gerundio («-ing») funciona como un nombre y puede ser el sujeto de la frase. El verbo va en singular:\n- «Studying for exams is exhausting.»\n- «Playing the guitar relaxes me.»\n- «Living abroad isn't always easy.»\n\nOjo: en español usamos el infinitivo («estudiar es agotador»), pero en inglés va en «-ing» (no «To study is…» en el habla normal). También va en «-ing» detrás de una preposición: «I'm good at cooking», «Thank you for coming».",
    "table": {
      "headers": [
        "Tiempo",
        "Forma",
        "Ejemplo"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Futuro",
          "will be able to",
          "I'll be able to drive."
        ],
        [
          "Present perfect",
          "have been able to",
          "I haven't been able to sleep."
        ],
        [
          "Con modal",
          "might / would like to be able to",
          "I'd like to be able to swim."
        ],
        [
          "Logro pasado",
          "was / were able to",
          "I was able to finish."
        ],
        [
          "Gerundio sujeto",
          "-ing + verbo singular",
          "Swimming is good for you."
        ]
      ]
    }
  },
